  "worked_solution": "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(x)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={((-4x)-1)}^{2}+{((2x-3)-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =(-4x-1)^{2}+(2x-8)^{2} \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}+8x+1+4{x}^{2}-32x+64 \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-24x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{\u2032}(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{\u2032}(x)=40x-24.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40x-24 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}(\\frac{3}{5})=40>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(x)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-24(\\frac{3}{5})+65} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{289}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(-4x,\\,2x-3)$ and $(1,\\,5)$ is $\\frac{17\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$",
